     "notes": {
      "text": "1...d5 is the classical answer: it controls e4 and c4, so White cannot keep a pawn on e4, stops d4-d5 and frees your queen's bishop. 1...Nf6 is a more flexible choice. 1...e5?! (Englund Gambit) is unsound, and 1...f5 (Dutch) can run into early tricks on the weak e8-h5 diagonal.",
      "credit": {
       "source": "Wikibooks, Chess Opening Theory/1. d4",
       "url": "https://en.wikibooks.org/wiki/Chess_Opening_Theory/1._d4",
       "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
       "licenceUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
       "authors": "Wikibooks contributors (see the page history)"
      }
