     "notes": {
      "text": "2.c4 offers a pawn. If White plays cxd5 you want to take back with a pawn and keep control of e4, so 2...e6 (or 2...c6, the Slav) is the usual way to decline. 2...dxc4 is playable but gives up e4. 2...Nf6 loses time after 3.cxd5 Nxd5 4.e4!, and 2...Bf5? (Baltic) is thought worse for Black.",
      "credit": {
       "source": "Wikibooks, Chess Opening Theory/1. d4/1...d5/2. c4",
